WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.478
Last lesson’s method only worked for exponentials, polynomials, sines and cosines.

2
00:00:07.478 --> 00:00:13.133
But what if the right side is one over cosine x, or tangent x?

3
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:18.513
Today we learn a universal method, variation of parameters.

4
00:00:18.513 --> 00:00:25.262
It works for any continuous right side and even for variable coefficients.

5
00:00:25.512 --> 00:00:34.826
The idea: turn the constants in the homogeneous solution C one y one plus C two y two into functions of x.

6
00:00:34.826 --> 00:00:38.604
Two unknown functions allow two conditions.

7
00:00:38.604 --> 00:00:44.227
The first: C one prime y one plus C two prime y two equals zero.

8
00:00:44.227 --> 00:00:52.838
The second comes from the equation: C one prime y one prime plus C two prime y two prime equals f.

9
00:00:53.088 --> 00:00:59.421
The determinant of this system is the Wronskian, which is non-zero.

10
00:00:59.421 --> 00:01:08.590
By Cramer’s rule, C one prime equals minus y two f over W, and C two prime equals y one f over W.

11
00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:17.380
An important warning: these formulas hold only when the coefficient of y double prime is one.

12
00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:21.350
Otherwise divide the equation by it first.

13
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.814
Example: y double prime plus y equals one over cosine x,

14
00:01:26.814 --> 00:01:31.842
on the interval from minus pi over two to pi over two.

15
00:01:31.842 --> 00:01:41.618
The homogeneous solutions are cosine x and sine x, and the Wronskian is cosine squared plus sine squared,

16
00:01:41.618 --> 00:01:42.828
which is one.

17
00:01:42.828 --> 00:01:44.318
Very convenient.

18
00:01:44.568 --> 00:01:49.145
C one prime equals minus sine x over cosine x,

19
00:01:49.145 --> 00:01:57.802
whose integral is the natural log of cosine x; on this interval the cosine is positive.

20
00:01:57.802 --> 00:02:04.668
C two prime equals cosine over cosine, which is one, with integral x.

21
00:02:04.668 --> 00:02:11.534
The particular solution is cosine x times log cosine x plus x sine x.

22
00:02:11.784 --> 00:02:14.444
Why does normalising matter?

23
00:02:14.444 --> 00:02:21.664
Take x squared y double prime minus two x y prime plus two y equals x cubed.

24
00:02:21.664 --> 00:02:26.794
Dividing by x squared gives f equal to x, not x cubed.

25
00:02:26.794 --> 00:02:31.259
The correct computation gives x cubed over two.

26
00:02:31.259 --> 00:02:40.094
If we forget to divide, we get x to the fifth over twelve, which does not solve the equation.

27
00:02:40.344 --> 00:02:47.137
The graph shows the particular solution cosine x times log cosine x plus x sine x.

28
00:02:47.137 --> 00:02:55.172
As x approaches pi over two the force grows without bound, but the solution itself stays bounded.

29
00:02:55.172 --> 00:03:04.118
The solution lives only on the interval containing the initial point, from minus pi over two to pi over two.

30
00:03:04.368 --> 00:03:11.138
Question: what is C two prime for y double prime plus y equals tangent x?

31
00:03:11.138 --> 00:03:19.485
Remember that y one is cosine x and the Wronskian is one, and substitute into the formula.

32
00:03:19.485 --> 00:03:23.102
Pause the video and choose your answer.
